# Target C-II — two-bin dominant-orbit mass sharpens shared outer energy

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Repository base:** `b47a3bed3ff59655497e33d34714fbfb8ba6bd66`  
**Status:** proof-only strengthening of the audited shared dominant/complement endpoint-retention theorem  
**Scope:** fully force-visible no-container/full-core Type-II branch  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The current shared-retention theorem controls the dominant exact `pi/3` atomic train
orbit by

\[
M\ge {4\Delta_2\over A},
\qquad
A=\sum_r M_r,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\]

using

\[
4\Delta_2\le\sum_r M_r^2\le M\sum_rM_r=MA.
\]

Near the current endpoint, the dominant orbit already contains almost all atomic mass,
so the last inequality is unnecessarily weak.  If `M=max_r M_r`, all other orbit
masses have total `A-M`, hence

\[
\sum_{r\ne *}M_r^2\le(A-M)^2.
\]

Therefore

\[
4\Delta_2\le M^2+(A-M)^2,
\]

which gives the stronger exact lower bound

\[
\boxed{
M\ge m_2(A):={A+\sqrt{8\Delta_2-A^2}\over2}.
}
\]

Propagating this through the already-audited shared endpoint-retention value function
raises the safe proof-only Type-II floor from `1.83279` to

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {q-k\over D}\ge1.83287.
}
\]

The new scalar crossing is approximately

\[
1.83287497466\ldots .
\]

Only the round value `1.83287` is claimed.

---

## 1. Atomic-orbit input

Use the audited train-phase notation.  Let `M_r>=0` be the absolute atomic train mass
in exact projective `pi/3` orbit `r`, and let `E_r` be the limiting strict-rhombus
crossing mass of that orbit.  Then

\[
E_r\le {M_r^2\over4},
\qquad
\sum_rE_r=\Delta_2,
\qquad
A:=\sum_rM_r.
\tag{TB.1}
\]

Let

\[
M:=\max_rM_r.
\]

Summing (TB.1),

\[
4\Delta_2\le\sum_rM_r^2.
\tag{TB.2}
\]

The same inequality with `sum M_r^2<=A^2` gives

\[
A\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}.
\tag{TB.3}
\]

---

## 2. Two-bin square bound

Isolate one orbit attaining `M`.  Since the residual orbit masses are nonnegative and
sum to `A-M`,

\[
\sum_{r\ne *}M_r^2
\le\left(\sum_{r\ne *}M_r\right)^2
=(A-M)^2.
\tag{TB.4}
\]

Hence

\[
4\Delta_2
\le M^2+(A-M)^2.
\tag{TB.5}
\]

On the low-`t` interval below, `A^2<8\Delta_2`.  The quadratic inequality (TB.5) has
roots

\[
{A\pm\sqrt{8\Delta_2-A^2}\over2}.
\]

The previously audited crude bound

\[
M\ge{4\Delta_2\over A}
\]

satisfies

\[
{4\Delta_2\over A}>{A\over2}
\]

whenever `A^2<8Delta2`.  Thus `M` lies on the upper branch of (TB.5), proving

\[
\boxed{
M\ge m_2(A):={A+\sqrt{8\Delta_2-A^2}\over2}.
}
\tag{TB.6}

No new phase geometry is used; this is only the exact extremal square allocation of
the residual atomic mass.

---

## 3. Replace `4 Delta2/U` in the complement-train ceiling

The exposed-hull argument from the dominant-orbit theorem gives

\[
A\le C,
\qquad
C:={T-q/2\over D}.
\tag{TB.7}
\]

The current matched train gap and Section-171 envelope give

\[
C_{50}\le C\le U,
\tag{TB.8}
\]

where

\[
C_{50}:=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0050,
\]

\[
U={9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta-{\kappa\over2},
\qquad
 t={q-k\over D},
\quad
\delta={d_{out}\over D},
\quad
\kappa={k\over D}.
\tag{TB.9}
\]

On `A>=2sqrt(Delta2)`, the function `m_2(A)` is nonincreasing because

\[
m_2'(A)
={1\over2}
\left(
1-{A\over\sqrt{8\Delta_2-A^2}}
\right)
\le0.
\tag{TB.10}
\]

Thus

\[
M\ge m_2(A)\ge m_2(C).
\tag{TB.11}
\]

The complete stopped-train density is

\[
{T\over D}=C+{t+\kappa\over2}.
\]

If `N_c` is the train density outside the dominant orbit windows, then

\[
N_c
\le C+{t+\kappa\over2}-m_2(C).
\tag{TB.12}
\]

Since

\[
{d\over dC}[C-m_2(C)]=1-m_2'(C)>0,
\]

and `C<=U`,

\[
\boxed{
N_c\le N_2(U):=
U+{t+\kappa\over2}-m_2(U).
}
\tag{TB.13}

This strictly improves the current ceiling

\[
U+{t+\kappa\over2}-{4\Delta_2\over U}.
\]

---

## 4. Insert the sharper ceiling into shared endpoint retention

The hostile-audited shared-retention theorem is otherwise unchanged.  Dominant-window
outer occurrences and complement outer-to-outer terminal occurrences share one route
capacity budget.  If `z` is retained dominant occurrence mass and `p` is retained
complement pair mass, then

\[
\boxed{
z+p\ge S:=\pi-N_2(U).}
\tag{TB.14}

Their disjoint actual edge-energy payments are

\[
F(z)=z-6\sin(z/6),
\]

\[
J(p)=2p-6\sin(p/3)
\qquad(0\le p\le1).
\]

Define the same infimal convolution

\[
\boxed{
H(S):=
\min_{\substack{z,p\ge0\\z+p=S}}
\{F(z)+J(p)\}.
}
\tag{TB.15}

On the present scalar interval its unique minimizer is interior, has `p<1`, and obeys

\[
F'(z)=J'(p).
\tag{TB.16}

Every survivor must satisfy

\[
\boxed{
\delta\ge H\!\left(
\pi-U-{t+\kappa\over2}+m_2(U)
\right).
}
\tag{TB.17}

---

## 5. The full feasible triangle still reduces to one boundary point

The scalar envelope gives

\[
2\delta+\kappa/2
\le R_{50}(t):={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_{50}.
\tag{TB.18}

Put

\[
S(t,\delta,\kappa)
=
\pi-U-{t+\kappa\over2}+m_2(U).
\]

### Topology direction

Since `partial U/partial kappa=-1/2`,

\[
{\partial S\over\partial\kappa}
=-{m_2'(U)\over2}>0
\tag{TB.19}

through the strict low-`t` range.  Since `H` is increasing, the contradiction margin
`H(S)-delta` is minimized at

\[
\boxed{\kappa=0.}
\tag{TB.20}

### Detour direction

Since `partial U/partial delta=-2`,

\[
{\partial S\over\partial\delta}
=2(1-m_2'(U)).
\tag{TB.21}

For `t<=1.83287`, every feasible `U` is below two.  On
`C50<=U<2`, the certificate verifies the conservative bound

\[
m_2'(U)>-1/8,
\]

so

\[
{\partial S\over\partial\delta}<9/4.
\tag{TB.22}

The value function `H` has marginal cost no larger than the cost of assigning new
retained mass entirely to the dominant variable.  Since `S<pi`,

\[
0\le H'(S)\le F'(\pi)=1-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}.
\tag{TB.23}

Therefore the upper Dini derivative obeys

\[
{d^+\over d\delta}[H(S)-\delta]
< {9\over4}\left(1-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)-1
< -0.69.
\tag{TB.24}

Thus maximal detour is the weakest point.  Combining with (TB.20),

\[
\boxed{
\kappa=0,
\qquad
\delta={R_{50}(t)\over2},
\qquad
U=C_{50}.
}
\tag{TB.25}

---

## 6. One-variable boundary problem

At (TB.25), the sharper dominant mass is the fixed constant

\[
\boxed{
M_{50}:=m_2(C_{50})
={C_{50}+\sqrt{8\Delta_2-C_{50}^2}\over2}.
}
\tag{TB.26}

The complement-train ceiling and required retained mass become

\[
N_0(t)=C_{50}+{t\over2}-M_{50},
\tag{TB.27}
\]

\[
S_0(t)=\pi-N_0(t)
=\pi-C_{50}-{t\over2}+M_{50}.
\tag{TB.28}

Let

\[
G_0(t):=H(S_0(t))-{R_{50}(t)\over2}.
\tag{TB.29}

Since `S_0'(t)=-1/2`, `R_50'(t)/2=9/4`, and `H'>=0`,

\[
G_0'(t)<0.
\tag{TB.30}

Thus there is at most one scalar crossing.

At `t=1.83287`, the independent certificate gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
M_{50}&=1.9046190334611455\ldots,\\
\delta_0&=0.0275520395462499\ldots,\\
S_0&=2.2201510733230236\ldots,\\
z_0&=1.640909064904494\ldots,\\
p_0&=0.579242008418530\ldots,
\end{aligned}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
G_0(1.83287)
=1.12854268\times10^{-5}>0.
}
\tag{TB.31}

The scalar zero is approximately

\[
1.83287497466\ldots .
\tag{TB.32}

Therefore:

### Theorem TB.1 — two-bin dominant-orbit floor

Every full-core Type-II survivor sequence in the stated proof-only scope satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}{q-k\over D}
\ge1.83287.
}
\tag{TB.33}

This supersedes the current `1.83279` shared-retention floor.

---

## 7. Frontier effect

At `U=C50`, the old and new dominant mass floors are

\[
{4\Delta_2\over C_{50}}
=1.8996387052992785\ldots,
\]

\[
M_{50}
=1.9046190334611455\ldots .
\]

Thus the two-bin square bound identifies about

\[
0.00498032816D
\]

additional trains as belonging to the dominant exact orbit.  Those trains are removed
from the complement-train ceiling and must be paid by the shared outer-energy
allocation.

No new rigidity, KKT-weight, or source-motion statement is used.  C-II remains open.
Further progress should now use more than the total residual atomic mass: the
`g=0.0050` minimax proof also constrains the three individual phase classes inside the
dominant orbit, information not used by (TB.6).

**Classification:** proof-only candidate pending certificate and hostile audit.
